hi there this is steve kaufman again uh  i'm going to do a little uh video here  on memory  this is a request from one of my viewers  wakako  um  what do i consider to be the role of  memory in language learning  i'd like to start with an example that i  got from um  oscar one of our link members in spain  we just had a conversation in spanish  and we talked about memory and he said  you know it's a bit like driving a car  when we drive a car  we do everything automatically because  we have  basically ingrained the the  habits the patterns all the things we  need to do in order to drive a car we do  them automatically and we can't remember  the last 10 minutes of our ride what we  did how we  you know signaled or turned or breaked  or changed gears or whatever it might be  and i think language learning is a bit  like that let me think of some other  things that relate to memory um i read  somewhere that they did a test with a  group of  two groups of 20 language learners  and the first  group was given a list of  whatever 20 30 words to learn  to study  and the second group was not given that  list then they were asked to read a text  and there was no difference in the  ability of these two groups to read  this text this was in the language that  they were learning and and so i mean  what was  being pointed out in that study was that  being confronted with a lot of things to  remember  we don't necessarily remember them very  well  um  you know i have the experience when i  read sometimes for my russian study i'll  read a play by chekhov  and at the beginning of the play they  introduce all of the  people who are in the play the general  and his daughter and so so-and-so and  his wife and  and of course as soon as i start reading  the play i can't remember who's who  i know that when i look up words in a  dictionary uh no sooner do i look up the  words then i've forgotten what the  meaning was  so  i think memory is not a very good thing  to rely on when speaking  sort of to review because it's a bit  like driving the car you have to be able  to produce these words and phrases  automatically on the fly  when we're driving i don't think we rely  on memory i don't think we try to  remember something we read in a manual  um  you know when i'm reading the play it's  only after i've read the play  quite a way through the play and i start  to become comfortable with the  characters in the play  occasionally referring back to the list  of characters that that i become  comfortable with who  who is who in the play  uh  similarly with  with uh words you know it's only when  i've seen the words in different  contexts that they start to stick  so  i think what we're dealing with in  language learning is more a matter of  establishing certain patterns in the  brain  so that initially things are new  primarily words and phrases and  gradually these become  patterns  uh and the way the patterns are formed  is either  it's i think it's a combination of  of uh you know the brain  kind of  absorbing a lot of input and starting to  form its own patterns based on what it  hears  but i think the brain also  tries to rely on some logic that the  brain has  and  one good example is  my favorite example of the third person  singular in the present tense of english  it's the only one that changes i go you  go he goes  uh he went they went uh you know but  he talks she talks so that always  changes  and yet i would think that amongst  even some very proficient speakers of  english even some native speakers of  english  he don't go  um  you know uh  people continue to make that mistake  because the the brain is saying  the pattern is that this should be the  same as all the others  and it's very difficult to remember by a  memory  that the third person singular is the  only one that's different  uh  so i see language learning more as a  matter of  of gradually  getting used to something and allowing  the brain to form the necessary  patterns and this requires a lot of  exposure  you know tables i have tried in german  in russian to memorize  the tables i mean i don't have it handy  here i'd show you i've printed out the  list i even tried  rather than having the list based you  know for every  say none i'm talking about declensions  of nouns  rather than having the list for  masculine feminine neuter in the  different cases i i actually tried to  write a table where i would write the  ending ooh  and or uh  whatever it might be oi and then all the  nouns and the different cases and  genders that correspond to that ending  so rather than starting with the oneness  all of this stuff would have  very short term  influence on me  so so where i end up is that  we're not talking memory so much in  language learning  uh i think what we're talking about is  two things  uh three in fact i get back to the what  i call the golden trinity of language  learning  attitude  time on task  and attentiveness  those are the three things not memory  memory doesn't make it into the  golden trinity so you want to learn the  language you like the language you think  you can do it  you can visualize yourself as a speaker  of the language so you got all of that  you're not afraid of it you're not  resisting it you're not asking why do  they do it that silly way so your  attitude is positive that's number one  number two you're willing to spend a lot  of time  listening reading listening while doing  the dishes reading when you have a  chance  on the internet newspapers television  whatever it may be you're willing to put  a lot and talking to people to put a lot  of time into the language so that's two  time so attitude time and the third one  is attentiveness  noticing things you have to notice now  how do we notice i mean we've all had  the experience that when westerners go  to let's say japan they think all the  japanese look alike  japanese people have told me that they  think all westerners look alike  because  initially they're just different so we  don't notice the individual differences  we just see they're different  and after having when i lived in japan  for nine years i found that foreigners  all looked alike and the japanese looked  quite different from each other because  i spent most of my time looking at  japanese people so so that i had  therefore  sort of a a background  against which to notice things  and the same is true in language  learning when we first hit the language  everything is very strange we don't  notice any we don't notice much except  that it's very strange and we start to  learn a few words again  my favorite approach is to repetitively  listen to limited content initially and  then i start to  notice things i start to you know save  words and phrases in link i review them  in flash cards i find the flashcarding  is good because i noticed not only the  word and the word ending but i noticed  the phrase that was captured and i i  might edit my phrase in link so that i'm  spending time on it it's all helping me  notice  and as i have more knowledge and more  familiarity as the brain has been  exposed to more and more of it i start  to notice things that i didn't notice  before  and so uh as we become first of all i'm  motivated to learn  i'm spending the time i'm trying  deliberately to notice i'm trying to  notice when i read i will underline  certain phrases i will underline certain  cases  uh i will review them in my flashcards  which i really like doing now that i  have them on my ipod touch it's become  just about my favorite activity i think  it's really neat doing that  using the words and phrases that were in  the text that i had just reviewed  uh so i'm cultivating this ability to  notice to be attentive to direct my we  have to he even for pronunciation we  have to hear it we have to notice how  people pronounce  but for words for rules of grammar for  all of these things i don't think we can  rely on memory  i think we have to rely on  trying to notice  within the context of a lot of exposure  and  of course that we're motivated so  memory i don't think is a big deal i  don't think people who have good  memories are good language learners i  don't think musicians are good language  learners i don't think engineers are  good language learners because they can  understand grammar i think it's a matter  of paying attention getting enough  exposure and really wanting  to do it so there you have it as i run  out of time that's my view on the role  of members
 

